package cajeta.concurrent;

import cajeta.lang.Optional;

/**
 * Source of values produced asynchronously. Each `next()` returns a
 * present `Optional``<T>` carrying the next value or an empty
 * `Optional``<T>` when the source is exhausted. Implementations are
 * free to park / suspend internally — `next()` is called from inside
 * a fiber context (e.g. an `async` method or a `Tasks``.runBlocking`
 * body) so blocking on a channel, lock, or I/O readiness is sound.
 *
 * ## Iteration pattern (v1)
 *
 * The user-side loop is explicit:
 *
 *     Optional<T> opt = iter.next();
 *     while (opt.isPresent()) {
 *         T x = opt.get();
 *         ...
 *         opt = iter.next();
 *     }
 *
 * A dedicated `for (T x in iter) { ... }` desugaring (mirroring the
 * array-iteration form `EnhancedForStatement` already provides) is the
 * planned v2.1 surface; the runtime contract is set by this interface
 * either way.
 *
 * ## Relationship to `Channel``<T>`
 *
 * `Channel``<T>` is naturally an AsyncIterator: its `receive()` already
 * returns `Optional``<T>` (present until the channel is closed and
 * drained, empty thereafter). A `Channel` can therefore be drained via
 * the loop above; binding it to a local of declared interface type
 * (`AsyncIterator<int32> iter = ch;`) requires the implements clause,
 * which we can wire onto `Channel``<T>` once the M5 value-return ripples
 * settle on async-updates.
 */
public interface AsyncIterator<T> {
    /**
     * Yield the next item, blocking the calling fiber if necessary,
     * or return empty when the source is exhausted. After the first
     * empty result, subsequent calls must remain empty (terminal
     * behavior, mirroring how `Channel``<T>.receive` behaves post-close).
     */
    Optional<T> next();
}
